            "authorAssociation": "MEMBER",
            "body": "> will throw error if called before any picker was called first.\r\n\r\nYes, because I think this is a reasonable behavior: throw error is something is assumed possible, but it is not.\r\n\r\n> Is there a way to check if there are pickers before resuming, or make it fail silently if there are no pickers?\r\n\r\nIt is possible to do not show the error with `pcall`. So something like this:\r\n\r\n```lua\r\nMiniPick.registry.resume = function() pcall(MiniPick.builtin.resume) end\r\n```\r\n\r\nNow executing `:Pick resume` will not show that error. In general, there could have been problems (like suppressing other errors or the extra care to preserve the output value), but by adjusting the user command it should not be a problem.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n@andradei, could you please explain a bit why this is a problem for you? Is it to only avoid the error after accidental `:Pick resume` soon after starting a new Neovim session? Or is there something else?",
            "bodyHTML": "<blockquote>\n<p dir=\"auto\">will throw error if called before any picker was called first.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p dir=\"auto\">Yes, because I think this is a reasonable behavior: throw error is something is assumed possible, but it is not.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p dir=\"auto\">Is there a way to check if there are pickers before resuming, or make it fail silently if there are no pickers?</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p dir=\"auto\">It is possible to do not show the error with <code class=\"notranslate\">pcall</code>. So something like this:</p>\n<div class=\"highlight highlight-source-lua notranslate position-relative overflow-auto\" dir=\"auto\" data-snippet-clipboard-copy-content=\"MiniPick.registry.resume = function() pcall(MiniPick.builtin.resume) end\"><pre class=\"notranslate\"><span class=\"pl-smi\">MiniPick</span>.<span class=\"pl-e\">registry</span>.<span class=\"pl-en\">resume</span> <span class=\"pl-k\">=</span> <span class=\"pl-k\">function</span>() <span class=\"pl-c1\">pcall</span>(<span class=\"pl-smi\">MiniPick</span>.<span class=\"pl-e\">builtin</span>.<span class=\"pl-e\">resume</span>) <span class=\"pl-k\">end</span></pre></div>\n<p dir=\"auto\">Now executing <code class=\"notranslate\">:Pick resume</code> will not show that error. In general, there could have been problems (like suppressing other errors or the extra care to preserve the output value), but by adjusting the user command it should not be a problem.</p>\n<hr>\n<p dir=\"auto\"><a class=\"user-mention notranslate\" data-hovercard-type=\"user\" data-hovercard-url=\"/users/andradei/hovercard\" data-octo-click=\"hovercard-link-click\" data-octo-dimensions=\"link_type:self\" href=\"https://github.com/andradei\">@andradei</a>, could you please explain a bit why this is a problem for you? Is it to only avoid the error after accidental <code class=\"notranslate\">:Pick resume</code> soon after starting a new Neovim session? Or is there something else?</p>",
            "bodyText": "will throw error if called before any picker was called first.\n\nYes, because I think this is a reasonable behavior: throw error is something is assumed possible, but it is not.\n\nIs there a way to check if there are pickers before resuming, or make it fail silently if there are no pickers?\n\nIt is possible to do not show the error with pcall. So something like this:\nMiniPick.registry.resume = function() pcall(MiniPick.builtin.resume) end\nNow executing :Pick resume will not show that error. In general, there could have been problems (like suppressing other errors or the extra care to preserve the output value), but by adjusting the user command it should not be a problem.\n\n@andradei, could you please explain a bit why this is a problem for you? Is it to only avoid the error after accidental :Pick resume soon after starting a new Neovim session? Or is there something else?",
